 ## Next charge date
 
-`computeNextChargeAt(previousChargeAt, interval)` adds one interval: 1 month for `monthly`, 3 for `quarterly`, 12 for `yearly`. The time of day is kept. All dates are UTC ISO strings.
+`computeNextChargeAt(previousChargeAt, interval, anchorDay)` adds one interval: 1 month for `monthly`, 3 for `quarterly`, 12 for `yearly`. The time of day is kept. All dates are UTC ISO strings.
 
-When the target month is shorter than the billing day, the date is clamped to the last day of that month (`addMonthsClamped`): a subscription started on January 31 is charged on February 28 (February 29 in leap years).
+Every subscription has a **billing day**: the day of month it started (`billingAnchorDay(started_at)`). Charges fall on that day. When a month is shorter than the billing day, the charge falls on the last day of that month, and the next one goes back to the billing day: a subscription started on January 31 is charged on February 28 (29 in leap years), March 31, April 30, May 31, and so on. It never drifts to the 28th.
 
-`advancePast(previousChargeAt, interval, now)` repeats this until the date is strictly after `now`; it is used when a subscription comes back from a pause.
+Callers always pass the anchor day, because `previousChargeAt` may itself have been clamped. Renewals (`runDueRenewals`) and resume (`advancePast`) both do.
+
+`advancePast(previousChargeAt, interval, now, anchorDay)` repeats this until the date is strictly after `now`; it is used when a subscription comes back from a pause.
 
